WEBVTT

00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:05.653
Here's a 100 GB file, and a laptop with 16 GB of memory.

00:00:05.853 --> 00:00:08.966
Ask Python to sort it, and it runs out of memory.

00:00:09.466 --> 00:00:12.721
Ask the Unix sort command, and it just… finishes.

00:00:12.921 --> 00:00:16.958
While it works, files pile up in the temp folder, and then they vanish.

00:00:17.558 --> 00:00:19.426
Those files are the whole trick.

00:00:19.926 --> 00:00:23.475
This is how you compute on data that's bigger than your memory.

00:00:27.025 --> 00:00:31.348
Picture memory as your desk, and the disk as a warehouse across town.

00:00:31.548 --> 00:00:38.341
The desk is small, but anything on it is instant. The warehouse holds everything, but every trip is slow.

00:00:38.541 --> 00:00:45.556
How slow? If reaching into memory took one second, a read from a fast SSD would take about fifteen minutes.

00:00:45.756 --> 00:00:48.717
A seek on a spinning hard drive: more than a day.

00:00:49.517 --> 00:00:56.070
There's one saving grace. A trip costs about the same whether you bring back one item, or a whole crate.

00:00:56.270 --> 00:01:00.884
So what matters isn't how many operations you do. It's how many trips you make.

00:01:01.484 --> 00:01:04.785
On paper, heapsort and merge sort are both N log N.

00:01:04.985 --> 00:01:10.309
But heapsort hops all over the file, and makes about four trips for every item it sorts.

00:01:10.509 --> 00:01:15.292
Merge sort sweeps through in long, straight lines, and needs dozens of times fewer.

00:01:15.492 --> 00:01:17.666
And bigger crates only widen the gap.

00:01:20.116 --> 00:01:23.811
Computer scientists turned this into a model with three numbers.

00:01:24.011 --> 00:01:25.939
N: how many items you have.

00:01:26.139 --> 00:01:27.988
M: how many fit in memory.

00:01:28.188 --> 00:01:30.728
B: how many come in one crate, called a block.

00:01:31.128 --> 00:01:39.490
Then one bold simplification: computation is free. The only cost is the number of blocks moved between disk and memory.

00:01:39.690 --> 00:01:44.745
Reading the whole file once costs N over B. That's our yardstick, and it's called a scan.

00:01:46.495 --> 00:01:51.160
The classic algorithm is external merge sort, and it works in two phases.

00:01:51.360 --> 00:01:58.684
Phase one: fill memory, sort it right there, which is free, and write it back to disk as a sorted chunk called a run.

00:01:58.884 --> 00:02:01.371
Repeat until you've been through the whole file.

00:02:02.371 --> 00:02:05.866
It still isn't sorted, but now it's made of sorted runs.

00:02:06.066 --> 00:02:09.523
Every block was read once and written once: two scans.

00:02:12.673 --> 00:02:14.330
Phase two: merge the runs.

00:02:14.530 --> 00:02:20.017
You already know how to merge two sorted lists: compare the fronts, take the smaller, repeat.

00:02:20.217 --> 00:02:27.625
Merge the runs in pairs, then pairs of pairs, until one is left, and every round reads and writes the whole file.

00:02:28.425 --> 00:02:30.656
But look at memory while that happens.

00:02:30.856 --> 00:02:38.286
To merge two runs, you only need the front block of each, plus one block for the output. The rest of the desk sits empty.

00:02:38.986 --> 00:02:44.029
So use it. Give every run its own one-block buffer, and merge them all at once.

00:02:44.229 --> 00:02:48.182
A small heap tracks which run has the smallest item at its front.

00:02:48.382 --> 00:02:50.553
Move that item to the output buffer.

00:02:50.753 --> 00:02:53.867
When the output block fills, write it out in one trip.

00:02:54.067 --> 00:02:57.823
When an input buffer runs dry, fetch that run's next block.

00:02:58.023 --> 00:03:02.017
Every block still makes exactly one trip in, and one trip out.

00:03:05.217 --> 00:03:09.727
How many runs fit at once? About one per block of memory: M over B.

00:03:09.927 --> 00:03:17.700
With 16 GB of memory and 1 MB blocks, that's over 16,000 runs, merged in a single pass.

00:03:19.850 --> 00:03:27.481
So the number of passes is still a logarithm, but its base is M over B instead of two, and that base is enormous.

00:03:27.681 --> 00:03:34.698
To sort 10 TB on this laptop, two-way merging needs ten rounds over the data. The wide merge needs one.

00:03:34.898 --> 00:03:41.064
In fact, one pass to form runs plus one pass to merge can sort about a quarter of a petabyte.

00:03:41.664 --> 00:03:50.224
And it's optimal: in 1988, Aggarwal and Vitter proved that no comparison sort can do asymptotically fewer transfers.

00:03:50.724 --> 00:03:56.575
In our simulation, that's the difference between almost a million trips, and about four thousand.

00:03:59.025 --> 00:04:01.559
Once you know this pattern, you see it everywhere.

00:04:01.759 --> 00:04:09.565
When a database has to sort more rows than its memory budget, Postgres reports external merge, and spills runs to disk.

00:04:09.765 --> 00:04:14.346
Big-data shuffles in MapReduce and Spark sort chunks, spill them, and merge.

00:04:14.546 --> 00:04:20.327
Storage engines like RocksDB write sorted files, and keep merging them in the background.

00:04:20.527 --> 00:04:29.317
Searching gets the same treatment: a B-tree gives each node hundreds of children, so any of a billion keys is three or four trips away.

00:04:29.517 --> 00:04:38.147
And the desk and warehouse are relative: the same model describes cache versus RAM, and GPU memory versus the rest of the machine.

00:04:39.597 --> 00:04:43.603
So, when data outgrows memory: count trips, not operations.

00:04:43.803 --> 00:04:46.785
Stream through the data, instead of hopping around it.

00:04:46.985 --> 00:04:51.601
Build runs as big as memory, then merge as many as memory allows.

00:04:52.101 --> 00:05:01.037
That's external merge sort: a logarithm with a huge base, which is why sorting a file bigger than your RAM usually takes just two passes.
